    thinking: "**Analyzing Full-Stack Integration**\n\nI\'m now investigating the full-stack architecture, focusing on the development setup. Specifically, I\'m examining the `package.json` file and noticing Vite\'s configuration for the dev server, which uses port 3000. It seems a custom Vite plugin or middlewares in `vite.config.ts` are viable or an Express app can be mounted.\n\n\n**Clarifying Middleware Integration**\n\nOkay, the environment constraints are clear now! I realize that I *must* use a `server.ts` entry point with Express. In development, I need to mount `vite.middlewares` there, and absolutely avoid mounting Express inside `vite.config.ts`. For deployment, the `start` script in `package.json` needs to be set to `node server.ts`. I am getting it now.\n\n\n**Confirming Script Modifications**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that `tsx` is available and correctly installed. Now, I\'m verifying the `server.ts` setup with Express and `vite.middlewares`. The intention is that during development, the `tsx` command would handle transpilation, and it\'s looking great.\n\n\n**Designing Complete Architecture**\n\nI am now designing the complete architecture with a focus on modularity. I\'m checking the necessary modules, specifically focusing on `src/types/watchdogs.ts`. This involves defining interception verdicts such as `ALLOW`, `BLOCK`, `REDACT_EXECUTE`, and `HONEYPOT_REROUTE`, along with cascade tiers like `TIER_1_HEURISTICS`, `TIER_2_VECTOR_CACHE`, and `TIER_3_GROQ_OVERSEER`. I am also structuring security policies, incorporating features like blocking PII extraction and enforcing read-only file systems.\n\n\n**Defining Audit Log Structure**\n\nNow, I\'m defining the structure for audit log entries. I have outlined data fields like `id`, `timestamp`, and `agentId`, along with `inputPayload`, `tierVerdict`, and `actionTaken`, which I\'ve specified as TypeScript. I\'m incorporating detailed threat categories and indicators. Furthermore, I\'ve integrated `dynamicParamRedaction`, `strictSqlInjection`, storage state metrics, and the agent context.\n\n\n**Elaborating Tiered Proxies**\n\nI\'m now elaborating on the tiered proxy structure. I\'m focusing on the components of each tier: Tier 1\'s heuristic regex filters and IP blocklists are primary; Tier 2 involves vector embeddings, cosine similarity, and Tier 3 utilizes GROQ for advanced semantic analysis. I am designing how each tier will operate and integrate.\n\n\n**Structuring Tiered Analysis**\n\nI\'m now detailing Tier 3\'s Groq/Gemini Overseer functionality. I\'m focusing on handling novel or ambiguous requests that are routed to Groq or Gemini Flash via `@google/genai` with a custom User-Agent. This will involve deep semantic inspection based on compiled dynamic policies to determine verdicts such as ALLOW, BLOCK, REDACT_EXECUTE, HONEYPOT_REROUTE, and whether to trigger context wiping.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n\n**Defining API Endpoints**\n\nI am now focusing on refining the Express API endpoints. I am clarifying each endpoint\'s function: `/api/proxy/evaluate` intercepts tool invocations; `/api/policies` manages policy configuration; `/api/telemetry` provides real-time statistics, and `/api/audit-logs` provides immutable records. There are endpoints for wiping agent context and honeypot interaction, too. I\'m also planning SDK snippets.\n\n\n**Designing React Components**\n\nI\'m now detailing the React frontend components. I\'m focusing on key elements. I am designing a clean `TopBar` with wordmark, nav, and live system status. I will create `ThreatTelemetry` with an analytics dashboard, and `PolicyToggles` for no-code policy switches and live prompt inspection. I\'m also planning `AttackSimulator`, and audit log management with `AuditLogDrawer`. Also, `CascadeTraceModal`.\n\n\n"
